If every check were at the low end, $I=\$500{,}000$, then a $C=\$50{,}000{,}000$ pool could theoretically write $N_{\max}=\frac{C}{I}
=
\frac{50{,}000{,}000}{500{,}000}
=
100$ such checks before reserves and follow-on capital. If every check were closer to $I=\$2{,}500{,}000$, the same pool would support about $N \approx \frac{50{,}000{,}000}{2{,}500{,}000}=20$ checks. The interview does not say this is the fund construction. The calculation simply makes the scale concrete: early-stage investing is a portfolio problem, but each company still requires concentrated operating attention.

